// logos-demo — a tour of the TeX-family logos provided by the logos package.
//
// The logos package defines three macros — \TeX, \LaTeX and \TeXish — that
// reproduce the famous, carefully-kerned wordmarks of the TeX family using only
// the engine's box, kern, raise/lower and \vbox primitives. This demo shows them
// in running prose, across the whole document type scale, and in a centred
// display line, then explains how each is built.
//
// document already imports logos internally (it uses \TeX/\LaTeX in its own
// prose), so the second \use is harmless — we name it explicitly here because
// this is the logos demo.

\use{document}
\use{logos}

\title {The TeX-family Logos}
\author {The texish engine}
\date {June 2026}
\maketitle

\section {The three logos}

The \texttt{logos} package gives any document the three wordmarks of the \TeX{}
family.
The original \TeX, Donald Knuth's typesetting system; \LaTeX, Leslie Lamport's
document format layered on top of it; and \TeXish, the mark of this engine — a
playful cousin that sets \TeX{} with a small slanted ``ish'' tucked against the
final letter.

What makes these logos special is not their letters but their spacing. Each one
overlaps and lowers its glyphs by precise amounts so that the word reads as a
single designed unit rather than three or five letters set side by side. Because
the construction is expressed in relative units (em-fractions and x-heights),
the kerning holds together no matter what size the logo is set at.

\section {Across the type scale}

The same three macros, set at every size in the document's type scale. Notice
that the lowered E in \TeX, the raised A in \LaTeX, and the slanted, raised
``ish'' in \TeXish all keep their proportions as the type grows:

\noindent{\normalsize \TeX\quad \LaTeX\quad \TeXish}

\noindent{\large \TeX\quad \LaTeX\quad \TeXish}

\noindent{\Large \TeX\quad \LaTeX\quad \TeXish}

\noindent{\LARGE \TeX\quad \LaTeX\quad \TeXish}

\noindent{\huge \TeX\quad \LaTeX\quad \TeXish}

\noindent{\Huge \TeX\quad \LaTeX\quad \TeXish}

\section {In a display line, and in context}

Set large and centred on a line of their own, the wordmarks make a tidy
colophon:

{\LARGE\centerline{\TeX\quad \LaTeX\quad \TeXish}}

They also behave inside ordinary markup. Here they are in \textbf{bold}: the run
\textbf{\TeX, \LaTeX{} and \TeXish}, and in \textit{italic}: the run
\textit{\TeX, \LaTeX{} and \TeXish} — the kerns ride along with the surrounding
font, since they are measured from the current glyphs.

\section {How each logo is built}

\subsection {\TeX}

\TeX{} is three letters with the middle one dropped and pulled tight against its
neighbours. The E is set in an \texttt{hbox} and lowered by half an x-height with
\texttt{lower} so its centre sits below the baseline; a negative \texttt{kern} of
about a sixth of an em pulls it leftward under the bar of the T, and a second,
smaller negative \texttt{kern} closes the gap before the X. The result is the
unmistakable T-E-X with the E nestled between the taller letters.

\subsection {\LaTeX}

\LaTeX{} extends \TeX{} by riding a small capital A high against the L, at the
cap height. Lamport's own trick — reproduced here — measures the height of a T
into a box register, then sets a seven-point A inside a \texttt{vbox} built ``to''
that height with \texttt{vss} filling beneath it. The \texttt{vss} pushes the
small A up to the top of the box, so its apex lines up with the tops of the
surrounding capitals. Negative kerns tuck the A back over the L, and the rest of
the word is simply \TeX.

\subsection {\TeXish}

\TeXish{} is this engine's own mark: it is exactly \TeX, followed by a small,
raised, slanted ``ish'' set in a seven-point oblique. The negative \texttt{kern}
draws the ``ish'' in against the X, and the \texttt{raise} lifts it about a fifth
of an em off the baseline, so it reads as a light suffix clinging to the wordmark
rather than a separate syllable on the line.

\vfill\eject
